.DS_Store
tmp/

.env

# .claude/ is symlinked from ~/.claude in the recommended install, so Claude Code
# writes all its runtime state (backups/, cache/, debug/, plugins/, projects/, …)
# in here. Ignore everything under .claude/ and explicitly allow only the tracked
# defaults — so present and future runtime folders never leak into git.
.claude/*

# Tracked defaults (directories — git recurses into re-included dirs)
!.claude/agents/
!.claude/commands/
!.claude/hooks/
!.claude/output-styles/
!.claude/rules/
!.claude/scripts/
!.claude/skills/
# ...but skills are allow-listed ONE BY ONE, unlike the dirs above.
#
# Why this one is different: `.claude/skills/` is a DROP-IN directory. Installing any
# third-party skill puts a new subdirectory here, and with the bare `!.claude/skills/`
# re-include above, `git add -A -- .claude` then sweeps it into a PUBLIC repo — which is
# exactly what happened: `graphify` plus three symlinks (`computer-use`, `orca-cli`,
# `orchestration`) pointing at a `.agents/` path that exists under ~/.claude but not here,
# so the repo shipped three DEAD links. `install.sh` discovers what to link with
# `git ls-files .claude`, so every consumer would have got them in their own ~/.claude.
#
# Adding a line here is the deliberate act of shipping a skill to every consumer.
.claude/skills/*
!.claude/skills/README.md
!.claude/skills/test-locators/

# Tracked defaults (files)
!.claude/MEMORY.md
!.claude/README.md
!.claude/claude-defaults.md
!.claude/settings.json
!.claude/settings.plugins.example.json
!.claude/settings.codex.example.json
